<section id="start" class="chapter" data-sc-act="flow"><div class="chapter-label"><span>01 / The beginning</span><span>2022 →</span></div><h2>Player one.<br/>Then, developer.</h2><p class="lead">I've played games all my life. Eventually, I started building them too.</p><p>I started studying Computer Science at UMT in August 2022 and began freelance Unity work in March 2023. Alongside games, I explored the browser through small experiments in input, rules, and feedback.</p><div class="experiment"><div><span class="tiny">A small experiment</span><h3>Leave your mark.</h3><p>Click or drag across the squares.<br/>Simple inputs. Something happens.</p><button id="clear-grid" type="button">Clear the canvas ↺</button></div><div id="paint-grid" role="group" aria-label="Interactive drawing grid">{Array.from({length:64},(_,i)=><button type="button" aria-label={'Paint square '+(i+1)} aria-pressed="false"/>)}</div></div><p class="caption">Inspired by my early etch-a-sketch project. A little illustration of where curiosity can lead.</p><div class="small-links"><a href="https://usmanbutt-dev.github.io/etch-a-sketch/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original drawing project ↗</a><a href="https://usmanbutt-dev.github.io/calculator/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Calculator ↗</a><a href="https://usmanbutt-dev.github.io/rock-paper-scissors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rock, paper, scissors ↗</a></div></section>
<section id="practice" class="chapter" data-sc-act="flow">
<div class="chapter-label"><span>02 / Work in practice</span><span>Clients / studios / product teams</span></div>
<h2>From my own ideas<br/>to shared goals.</h2>
<p class="lead">Freelance work gave me real client goals to build around. Studio and product teams brought multiplayer games, leadership, and AI-powered applications into that work.</p>
<div class="experience">
<div><time>Jun – Sep 2026</time><h3>Termnl Tech</h3><p><strong>AI &amp; Full-Stack Engineer Intern</strong><br/>Built a RAG platform, developed React Native features, and worked with the team on testing and debugging.</p></div>
<div><time>Apr – Jun 2026</time><h3>Axiolink Game Studio</h3><p><strong>Head of Game Development</strong><br/>Led development direction and team coordination for Unity projects, with hands-on C# engineering.</p></div>
<div><time>Sep 2025 – Apr 2026</time><h3>AstroApe Studios</h3><p><strong>Unity Developer</strong><br/>Built real-time multiplayer features and optimized performance, working alongside artists and developers.</p></div>
<div><time>Mar 2023 – Present</time><h3>Freelance · Fiverr</h3><p><strong>Unity Developer</strong><br/>Build VR and multiplayer experiences around client goals using Unity, VR Interaction Framework, Final IK, and Photon PUN 2.</p></div>
</div>
<article class="project">
<div class="project-heading"><h3>Making documents searchable through conversation</h3><span class="status">Termnl Tech</span></div>
<p><strong class="project-summary">A web application that lets people ask questions across connected data sources and uploaded documents.</strong> It uses retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to find relevant information for an AI-generated answer.</p>
<p><strong class="project-summary">My contribution:</strong> I built the full-stack platform and designed its retrieval pipeline, using OpenAI for answer generation and Groq for reranking and intent classification. I added Redis caching for repeated queries.</p>
<p>I also developed React Native Android features, including prompt-driven image generation, and worked with the team on QA and debugging.</p>
<div class="project-footer"><span>React · Node.js / Express · MongoDB · Redis · BullMQ · OpenAI / Groq</span><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="/Muhammad-Usman-Butt-Resume.pdf">Read my resume ↗</a></div>
</article>
<p>Across these roles, the common thread has been making different parts work together: players in a shared world, an app and its services, or a question and the information behind an answer.</p>
</section>
<section id="tools" class="chapter" data-sc-act="flow"><div class="chapter-label"><span>03 / Better tools</span><span>Open source</span></div><h2>Sometimes the thing<br/>to build is a better tool.</h2><p>Working in Unity also made me interested in the tools around it: how an editor connects to other applications, and how developers interact with their projects.</p><p id="build-methods"><strong class="project-summary">How I work with AI.</strong> For the projects marked vibe-coded, I designed the systems and used AI to write the implementation. Directing that work has helped me become more confident working with coding agents and AI development workflows.</p><article class="project"><div class="project-heading"><h3>Antigravity for Unity</h3><span class="star-count" aria-label="25 GitHub stars, recorded 15 September 2026"><span aria-hidden="true">&#9733;</span><strong>25</strong><span>GitHub stars<small>Recorded 15 Sep 2026</small></span></span></div><p><strong class="project-summary">An open-source bridge between Unity and Antigravity.</strong> It lets developers open Unity scripts at the right line in Antigravity and keep project configuration in sync.</p><figure class="artwork"><img src="/images/antigravity.png" alt="Repository artwork connecting the Unity and Antigravity symbols" width="1024" height="512" loading="lazy" data-sc-parallax="0.04"/><figcaption>Project artwork from the repository</figcaption></figure><p class="caption"><strong>My role:</strong> I designed the integration; AI wrote the implementation. <a href="#build-methods">Vibe-coded</a>.</p><div class="project-footer"><span>Unity Editor · C# · UPM</span><a href="https://github.com/usmanbutt-dev/antigravity-unity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the repository ↗</a></div></article><article class="project mcp"><div class="project-heading"><h3>Unity MCP</h3><span class="status">Paused</span></div><p><strong class="project-summary">A bridge that lets AI assistants work with the Unity Editor.</strong> It exposes tools for inspecting and changing scenes, objects, and assets through the Model Context Protocol (MCP). I paused development when Unity introduced its own MCP tooling.</p><div class="flow-diagram" aria-label="AI client connects through MCP to Unity Editor"><span>AI client</span><b aria-hidden="true">↔</b><span>MCP</span><b aria-hidden="true">↔</b><span>Unity Editor</span></div><p class="caption">An illustration of the connection, not a running session.</p><p class="caption"><strong>My role:</strong> I designed the editor-tool interface; AI wrote the implementation. <a href="#build-methods">Vibe-coded</a>.</p><div class="project-footer"><span>C# · JSON-RPC · Editor tooling</span><a href="https://github.com/usmanbutt-dev/unity-mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the code ↗</a></div></article></section>
<section id="win" class="chapter award" data-sc-act="flow"><div class="chapter-label"><span>04 / A team win</span><span>GIKI SOFTCOM · 2026</span></div><div class="award-number" aria-label="First place">1<span>st</span><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 5L60 32L88 20L74 45L98 60L69 65L72 95L50 77L27 95L31 65L3 60L26 45L12 20L40 32Z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/></svg></div><h2>Built together.<br/>First place.</h2><p class="lead">Our team won first place in Game Development at GIKI SOFTCOM in 2026.</p><p><strong class="project-summary">A one-button Unity game with attacks, gravity switching, and generated level chunks.</strong> I worked on the game as part of the team. This was a shared result.</p><div class="mechanics"><span>Tap → attack</span><span>Hold → charge</span><span>Double tap → flip gravity</span></div><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/usmanbutt-dev/SoftcomProject" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore the game project ↗</a></section>
<section id="create" class="chapter" data-sc-act="flow"><div class="chapter-label"><span>05 / More possibilities</span><span>Create / extend</span></div><h2>Tools that leave<br/>room for other ideas.</h2><p>That interest in how systems fit together extends beyond Unity: into a writing tool that connects ideas, and a media app that can support new sources through extensions.</p><article class="project"><div class="project-heading"><h3>ArcSmith</h3><span class="status">Open source</span></div><p><strong class="project-summary">A visual narrative editor for writers to connect story elements.</strong> A graph workspace, project saving, undo/redo, and a local automation API support the authoring process.</p><figure class="artwork"><img src="/images/arcsmith.png" alt="ArcSmith promotional artwork showing a stylized narrative graph" width="945" height="468" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Repository promotional artwork</figcaption></figure><p class="caption"><strong>My role:</strong> I designed the authoring system; AI wrote the implementation. <a href="#build-methods">Vibe-coded</a>.</p><div class="project-footer"><span>React · TypeScript · Tauri / Rust</span><div class="index-links"><a href="https://usmanbutt-dev.github.io/ArcSmith/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Project website ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/usmanbutt-dev/ArcSmith" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Repository ↗</a></div></div></article><article class="project"><div class="project-heading"><h3>NijiStream</h3><span class="status">Extension-based app</span></div><p><strong class="project-summary">A media app powered by JavaScript source extensions.</strong> I used NijiStream to learn how to build an extension-based application. Its Flutter interface, library, and playback connect to JavaScript source extensions through a runtime bridge.</p><div class="extension-map"><div class="extension-core"><strong>NijiStream</strong><span>Interface · Library · Playback</span></div><div class="connector" aria-hidden="true">↕</div><div class="extension-bridge">Dart ↔ JavaScript bridge</div><div class="connector" aria-hidden="true">↕</div><div class="extension-sources"><span>Search</span><span>Details</span><span>Video sources</span></div></div><p class="caption">Architecture illustration based on the repository.</p><p class="caption"><strong>My role:</strong> I designed the app and extension architecture; AI wrote the implementation. <a href="#build-methods">Vibe-coded</a>.</p><div class="project-footer"><span>Flutter · Dart · JavaScript · SQLite</span><div class="index-links"><a href="https://usmanbutt-dev.github.io/NijiStream/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Project website ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/usmanbutt-dev/NijiStream" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Repository ↗</a></div></div></article><div class="resolution"><span class="tiny">Different projects. A common interest.</span><p>How we <strong>play</strong>.<br/>How we <strong>create</strong>.<br/>What happens <strong>next</strong>.</p><a href="/projects/">There’s more in the project index ↗</a></div></section>
<section id="about" class="chapter" data-sc-act="flow"><div class="chapter-label"><span>06 / The person</span><span>Lahore, Pakistan</span></div><h2>Still curious.<br/>Still building.</h2><p>I'm Muhammad Usman Butt, based in Lahore, Pakistan. I graduated with a BS in Computer Science from UMT in August 2026. Games were my starting point; building tools, applications, and AI integrations has given me more ways to turn an idea into something people can use.</p><p class="caption">BS Computer Science, UMT · Aug 2022–Aug 2026 · CGPA 3.50/4.00.<br/>Game-development community work with UMT ACM and GDGoC UMT.</p><p class="lead">I'm interested in software engineering roles across Unity and game development, full-stack applications, and applied AI.</p><div class="closing"><span class="tiny">The next chapter</span><h2>Have a role in mind?<br/>Let's talk<span class="blue">.</span></h2><a class="email" href="mailto:buttu7666@gmail.com">buttu7666@gmail.com ↗</a><div class="small-links"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="/Muhammad-Usman-Butt-Resume.pdf">Resume ↗</a><a href="https://github.com/usmanbutt-dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/usmanbutt-dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a></div></div></section>
